.\"                                      Hey, vim: ft=nroff
.TH SCREENATOR 1 "October 29, 2026"
.SH NAME
Screenator \- vibe-coded screen annotation tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B screenator
.RI [ options ]
.br
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBScreenator\fP lets you draw annotations over all your running applications.
It can run in the background and be activated on demand. The drawing stays on
screen as long as you want, and you can continue to use your applications
while it is visible.
.br
\fBScreenator\fP is a fork of Gromit-MPX 1.4.2. It adds a small draggable
on-screen icon whose left/right click opens a menu with the drawing tools
(pen, marker, shapes, text and eraser) and the settings (line colour,
thickness, eraser size, opacity, shape fill, text font and floating icon
size). The same menu is available from the system tray icon.
.br
The settings indicator is a ring around the floating icon. The inner white
ring is divided into nine equal sectors, one for each line thickness (1, 2,
3, 4, 5, 6, 12, 24 and 48 pixels), each with a one pixel black stroke. Starting at
angle 0 and going counter clockwise, the sectors up to and including the
selected thickness are filled magenta. The outer six pixel ring has the
line colour in its upper half and the shape fill colour in its lower half; when
shapes are not filled, the lower half shows twelve black and white radial
stripes. Both halves have a one pixel black stroke. Left or right click on the
ring opens the same menu as the floating icon. "Show settings indicator on
Floating Icon" in
Settings
the menu turns it off and on; it is on by default and is disabled while the
floating icon is hidden.
.br
The Marker draws a highlighter stroke with a fixed 32 pixel width, using the
current line colour. Its opacity is 40% by default and is independent of the
global pen opacity. The Marker Opacity menu with 10%, 20%, 30%, 40%, 50%, 60%,
70%, 80% and 90% is available from the app menu and tool bar; the choice is
remembered across restarts. Selecting the Marker switches the line
colour to yellow; leaving the Marker or exiting painting restores the colour
that was active before. The mouse wheel or the stylus first side button changes
its colour.
.br
While painting mode is active, the mouse wheel steps through the line colours,
including the custom colour, or the shape fill colours over the Paint Bucket.
In Screen mode, with the Eraser tool selected, the wheel changes the eraser size instead.
There is no long-right-click menu; middle clicks never erase. Right click
accepts typed text first, commits visible
handles on another click, and starts erasing on a later click. In Screen mode, with Eraser
selected, right click or stylus button 2
changes its size. The change is the same as picking it from
the menu, so it is kept when another tool is selected and is shown in the
settings indicator and the menus.
.br
The Timer tool (F12) is available in Screen Annotation mode only. It releases
the input for the Timer Delay set in Settings >
General, so you can switch to another application. Other tools cannot be
selected during the countdown. When it ends, the Pen tool becomes active. If
the pointer grab is blocked when the timer ends, for
example by another application's context menu, Screenator freezes the current
screen as a temporary background so the tool can draw over it; exiting the
tool removes the frozen background.
.br
The Arrow tool sits directly after the Eraser. The Shapes submenu contains
the Marker, Line, Spline, Polygon, Triangle, Ellipse, Rounded Rectangle, Paint
Bucket, Math Function and Parallel Lines tools.
Shapes are drawn by dragging from the start point to the end point. The line
and the arrow follow the drag direction, which snaps to 0, 30, 45, 60, 90 and
so on while Ctrl is held; without it the direction follows the pointer. The
arrow adds a triangular head at the end point by default and stays easy to see
even with a thin line. A square handle offset from the middle of the line
changes the heads: pressing it resets to none and dragging away cycles start,
end and both. A placed line or arrow stays editable: each end point has a
round handle, and dragging the line itself moves the whole shape; clicking
outside, double-clicking or right-clicking commits it.
Ellipses, rounded rectangles, polygons and triangles can be filled using the
"Shapes Fill Color" submenu; the default is no fill and green is its first
colour. Their outlines are drawn outside the shape border, so a thick stroke
never covers the inside of the shape. Both the line colour and the shape fill
colour have a "Custom..." entry that opens a colour chooser; the custom colour
starts as magenta and is remembered.
.br
The "Paper" submenu holds the paper tools. "Actions" sits below Shapes and
holds Switch Mode (F3), Undo, Redo, Clear Screen, Screenshot, Show toolbar
(F10), Show Floating Icon (F9) and Show Hints. Toggle Visibility and Ctrl+T
are available only in Screen mode; the menu item and toolbar icon are absent
in Paper mode. Ctrl+O opens a PDF in Paper mode. Switch
Mode turns Paper mode on or off and is also the first item in the
toolbar. Plain, Ruled and Graph are white
pages (with blue writing lines for Ruled and a grey square grid for Graph), and
selecting PDF File opens the desktop's own file chooser (KDE, GNOME, etc.).
"Reset Page or PDF File" in the Paper menu or the tool bar's Paper Type menu resets
the current Plain, Ruled or Graph paper to one empty page, or opens another PDF
when the current paper is PDF.  If the current paper has unsaved changes,
Screenator asks whether to save it in the Documents folder first. Selecting a
paper type, or opening a PDF, switches to Paper mode
automatically and brings the Paper window to the front.  Switching between
Screen and Paper always exits the current tool and leaves Cursor selected.
The same applies when selecting a paper type, resetting a paper or opening a
PDF.
Every type keeps its own pages, drawings and loaded PDF while
another type is shown, so switching back restores them without a save prompt.
Switching paper types keeps the active tool.
The selected paper type and PDF file are remembered across restarts.  Each PDF
file also remembers the page it was last closed on, whether reopened from
Screenator or from the desktop.  Paper saves show a progress overlay until the
PDF is complete, then the saved path.  Saving a screenshot shows a confirmation
with the saved path.
.PP
Paper mode uses a normal managed window rather than the fullscreen Screen
overlay, so other applications and dialogs can cover it and Alt+Tab works
normally. Ctrl+W switches to Window mode: the paper page width fits a normal
resizable window automatically and the desktop panel can appear above it.
The drawing tool and its cursor are active only inside the Paper window, and
no global pointer grab is taken, so resizing, the window frame and other
applications keep working normally. Ctrl/Alt shortcuts are received by the
focused Paper window as ordinary key events instead of being grabbed globally.
Pressing it again returns to fullscreen. Ctrl+D leaves fullscreen into a
borderless, non-resizable window covering the monitor, so the desktop panel
stays above the page; pressing it again returns to fullscreen. In every Paper
presentation (fullscreen, Ctrl+D and Window mode), the tool and cursor are
limited to the Paper window, and Ctrl/Alt shortcuts are received only while it
is focused.
Opening a PDF with Screenator (Open With or "screenator file.pdf") starts
directly in Paper mode with that PDF; if an instance is already running, the
PDF is forwarded to it. The desktop entry registers Screenator as a PDF
handler. While Paper mode is
active, the page is drawn
below
the annotations and above the other windows, takes the mouse and keyboard so
clicks and typing do not reach the desktop, and stays when the screen is
cleared. Ctrl+S, or the Save toolbar button, saves only the current paper if
it has changed, automatically to the Documents folder as
Screenator-plain-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.pdf, Screenator-ruled-..., Screenator-graph-...
or Screenator-file-... for opened PDF files (with a numeric suffix if that name
exists), and shows the saved path. Drawings are vector paths,
text is saved as text, and the original PDF pages and paper backgrounds stay
vector. Undo and redo keep drawings and text vector as well. Eraser strokes
over empty space leave the vector layer untouched; when ink is erased, the
remaining drawings stay vector and text stays text. On exit Screenator
offers once to save every changed paper; switching
paper types does not show a save prompt. Ctrl+B and Ctrl+A insert a page before or after the current page in
Plain, Ruled and Graph. With
Continuous scrolling on (the default), the selected paper is shown as one
continuous vertical document with a grey gap between its pages, and Up/Down
move smoothly through all of them. Page Up/Page Down move to the previous or
next page and Home/End jump to the first or last page. With it off, pages are
shown one at a time; scrolling moves within the current page and moves to the
next or previous page at the top or bottom edge. In Paper mode, holding the mouse middle button or
stylus side button 1 pans the
paper and shows a hand
cursor; holding the right mouse button or stylus side button 2 selects the
Eraser and shows the eraser cursor. Eraser and Paint Bucket panning use a 200 ms delay; other tools pan immediately, and both work
whether a tool is active or not; releasing the right button or stylus button 2
restores the previous tool. A middle click accepts typed text or commits
shape/text handles before panning. Right click accepts text, commits its
handles on the next click, then starts erasing on a later click. The mouse wheel
scrolls the pages, except while a placed shape or text shows its handles: then
the wheel and stylus button 1 change its colour. While text is being typed, the
wheel and stylus side button 1 change the text colour. Right click, stylus side
button 2 or mouse button 2 accepts the text like Enter. F10 shows or hides a tool bar in both Screen and Paper modes; by
default it is horizontal along the top edge, and Settings > General can make
it vertical along the right edge instead. Settings > Toolbar chooses the
visible icons and their order. The bar has Switch Mode first, then
Cursor, Pen, Text, Eraser, Arrow,
Timer, Shapes, Undo, Redo, Save, Paper Type, Insert Page Before, Delete Page,
Insert Page After, Current Page, Go to Page, Clear Screen, Toggle Visibility
(Screen mode only),
Eraser Size, Line Color,
Thickness,
Fill Color, Opacity, Marker Opacity, Settings, Show Hints and Quit last. The Timer icon
appears only in Screen mode. Toggle Visibility and its icon are absent from
the menu and tool bar in Paper mode. There is a 10 px
Paper Type selects a paper type; the
insert-page buttons insert a page in an active Plain, Ruled or Graph paper and
are hidden in Screen mode. Save saves the current changed paper to Documents,
or takes a screenshot in Screen mode. Quit uses quit.png. The selected tool is
highlighted.
Drag
any icon
or the gaps between them to move the bar; a short click still selects the tool.
Cursor exits the current tool and is available in both modes. In Paper mode
with no active tool, the middle mouse button or stylus side button 1 pans, the
wheel scrolls, and the right button or stylus side button 2 erases. The bar
also has a
Settings button near its end. The bar stays open when a tool or a menu
entry is chosen, its icons show tooltips, its direction and icon size
(16 to 64 px) are set in Settings > General, its visible icons and order are
set in Settings > Toolbar, its shortcuts are set in Settings > Shortcuts, and
its open/closed state is remembered.
Settings > Paper also sets continuous scrolling and the
paper size: Screen, A4 portrait, A4 landscape, or Custom width and height in
millimetres. A4 and Custom pages always take the current screen or Paper
window width, with their height following the paper ratio. The size applies to
Plain, Ruled and Graph pages and scales their existing drawings; PDF pages
keep the PDF's own size. Screen-size pages follow the Paper window's width and
height in Window mode, and return to the display size in fullscreen. Window
mode scales the canvas uniformly to the window, preserving shape proportions.
The drawing stays anchored to the top when the window gets taller and to the
left when it gets wider. Continuous PDF pages are
separated by a grey gap so their edges stay visible. Screenator always starts
on the normal desktop; unsaved paper pages are not restored.
.br
The Spline tool is placed click by click instead: every click adds a node, the
nodes are joined by a smooth Bezier curve, and dragging a node moves it. Every
node has two round Bezier handles; dragging one mirrors the other so the curve
stays smooth, and holding Ctrl while dragging moves only that side, which gives
the node a corner. Click the last node again, double-click or right-click to
finish the curve.
.br
The Polygon tool uses the first click as its centre; dragging then sets the
radius and rotation around that centre, while a plain click keeps a default
size. The shape stays editable with six handles: the square handle controls
the number of sides from 3 to 16 (pressing it resets the shape to 3 sides;
moving away from it in any direction adds sides and moving back towards it
removes them, down to 3 at the handle), one round handle sets the radius, one
sets the rotation, one moves the inner vertices outwards until the shape
becomes a regular polygon, and four corner handles scale it in X and Y after
rotation; the inner handle snaps to the flat edge when it gets close, so a
regular polygon is easy to hit. Drag inside the shape to move it.
Clicking empty space commits it and places the next one; double-click or
right-click commits it.
.br
The Triangle tool places the next vertex with each click. A line follows the
pointer after the first click, and the remaining sides follow after the second
click. Dragging does not place a point; the line keeps following the pointer
until the next click. The third click fixes the last vertex and closes the
triangle. The triangle keeps three round vertex handles, so it
can be stretched and skewed freely; holding Ctrl while dragging a vertex
snaps the angle at that vertex to 30, 45, 60 or 90 degrees. Drag inside the
triangle to move the whole shape. Clicking empty space commits it and places
the next one, and a double-click or right-click commits it.
.br
The Ellipse and Rounded Rectangle stay editable after they have been dragged
out. Every placed shape keeps a square size handle on each of its four corners
and a round rotation handle above it, and dragging inside the shape moves it;
rotation is free, and holding Ctrl snaps it to 0, 30, 45, 60, 90 and so on.
The Rounded Rectangle also keeps a roundness handle on its top edge:
dragging it away from the corner rounds the corners more, and dragging it
towards the corner makes them sharper. While dragging an ellipse or rounded
rectangle, hold Ctrl to make a circle or square. Clicking outside the shape,
double-clicking or right-clicking commits it.
.br
The Math Function tool opens a dialog for the expression and the range. The
Rectangular mode plots y = f(x) over an x range and takes a function of x; the
Polar mode plots r = f(theta) over a theta range, where theta is reserved for
the angle and the default range is 0 to 2*pi, a full circle. "pi" and "e" are
the constants pi and Euler's number, and multiplication can be implied
("2pi*x"). A preset drop-down fills the expression with "cos(2*pi*x)",
"sin(2*pi*x)", "unit(x)", "delta(x)", "rect(x)", "e^(x)", "log(x)",
"1/x", "sin(2*pi*x)/(2*pi*x)" or "(cos(2*pi*x)+2)*cos(20*pi*x)"; in polar
mode the same presets use theta instead of x. unit(x) is the unit step,
delta(x) is the Dirac delta, drawn as an arrow at the point where its argument
is zero, and rect(x) is a rectangle pulse that is 1 for |x| <= 1/2 and 0
elsewhere. The defaults are "cos(2*pi*x)" from x = -2 to
x = 2 and "cos(3*theta)" from theta = 0 to 2*pi; each mode keeps its own
expression and range. The plot is drawn inside a frame that is dragged out
like the other shapes and is scaled to fill it, so the corner handles also set
its amplitude; polar plots use one scale for both axes, so the curve stays
round in a non-square frame. The plot can be moved, resized and rotated with the usual
handles. Selecting the tool from the Shapes menu asks for the expression
first, and choosing it again changes the expression; a plot that is still
editable updates at once. A point where the
expression is undefined is filled in with its limit when both sides agree, so
"sin(2*pi*x)/(2*pi*x)" is drawn through x = 0; a real pole such as "1/x" or "tan(x)"
breaks the curve instead.
.br
The Parallel Lines tool draws between 2 and 40 evenly spaced vertical lines
with the current line thickness. The square handle outside the left edge sets
their number: pressing it resets the shape to two lines and moving away adds
more. The square handle outside the right edge changes the arrowheads on the
lines: pressing it resets to none and moving away adds them in the order top,
bottom and both; the handle shows the current mode. The lines can be moved,
resized and rotated with the usual handles.
.br
The Random Signal tool draws a random waveform. A single click places a
default-size signal centred on the click; dragging out a frame sets its size.
The square handle outside the left edge resets the smoothness to 1 (sharpest);
dragging it away in any direction raises it up to 16 (smoothest). The default
is 5. Clicking the signal without dragging generates a new signal with a new
seed. The frame can be moved, resized and rotated with the usual handles.
.br
The Paint Bucket fills the closed area under the click with the Shapes Fill
Color. In Screen and Paper modes, the mouse wheel or stylus Button 1 cycles
the fill color, regardless of the wheel or stylus setting.
In Paper mode, short-click stylus Button 1 or middle mouse to change color;
hold and drag to pan after a brief 200 ms delay and a small movement, including while hovering.
The area must be enclosed by drawn strokes and must not reach the screen
edge; otherwise nothing is filled, so clicking outside a shape cannot flood the
whole screen. With None selected the bucket erases the enclosed fill instead,
leaving the strokes around it. The tool shows a bucket cursor.
.br
The eraser size can be set to 20, 40, 60 or 120 pixels from the menu. The
erasing area and the circular eraser cursor both match the selected size.
.br
The Text tool lets you write directly on the screen. Click where the text
should start and type: a small borderless editor appears at that position,
using the pen colour and the font selected by "Change Font". The text is
centred vertically on the click point. The text and its blinking caret are
drawn above the drawings and the paper page, so they stay visible over them.
The middle mouse button does not erase in Screen mode and pans in Paper mode;
it accepts typed text first. Press
Enter to place the text or Escape to cancel it. In Screen mode,
a left click or stylus tip while the editor is open accepts the current text,
commits its handles and starts a new one at the click position. A placed text
stays editable: drag it to
move it, drag a corner handle to change the font size, and drag its round
handle to rotate it.
.br
The pen, font and floating icon position settings are remembered in
.I ~/.config/Screenator/screenator.ini
and can be restored to their defaults with the "Reset app settings and
drawings" entry in Settings. Actions, below Shapes, holds Switch Mode, undo,
redo, clear, visibility, screenshots and the view options.
Screenator starts on the normal desktop with Plain selected for the next
Paper session. Remembered PDF files, their pages and paper-size settings are
kept. Save the pages with Ctrl+S before quitting Paper mode if needed; the
exit confirmation also offers to save every changed paper.
.br
Every dialog has a title bar with a close button and a visible border, and is
shown above the drawings.
.br
The "Settings" item opens a tabbed dialog. Its General tab holds "Save
annotations on exit", "Change Font", "Show settings indicator on Floating
Icon", timer delay, "Reset app settings and drawings" and "Open Configuration
Folder"; its Paper tab holds continuous
scrolling and paper size; its Toolbar tab sets the tool bar direction, icon
size, visible icons and their order; its Floating Icon tab sets the floating
icon size and position; and its Shortcuts tab edits every shortcut.
The tool bar is Horizontal
on the top edge by default; choosing
Vertical places it on the right edge. The tool bar and floating icon are
visible by default.
The Floating Icon tab offers Top Right (the default), Top Left, Bottom Right
and Bottom Left; the choice is saved across restarts. The floating icon size is
16x16, 32x32, 48x48 (the default), 64x64, 128x128 or 256x256; the settings
ring doubles in width at 128x128 and triples at 256x256.
The Opacity menu offers 10% to 100% in 10% steps; the separate Marker Opacity
menu offers 10% to 90%.
"Reset app settings and drawings" deletes the configuration file and saved
drawings, clears every paper page, and returns to red, 6 px, 100% opacity, no
shape fill, Arial Bold 24, a 60 px eraser, a 48x48 icon, a visible 32 px
horizontal tool bar with all icons and a 2 s Timer Delay. Beside it, "Open
Configuration Folder" opens ~/.config/Screenator, and the folder path is shown
below the buttons.
"Actions", below "Shapes" in the main menu, holds Switch Mode, Undo, Redo,
Clear Screen, Screenshot, Show toolbar (F10), Show Floating
Icon (F9) and Show Hints. The floating icon and tool bar
stay clickable while a tool is selected; hovering them shows the normal
pointer and suspends drawing. With "Save
annotations on exit" on (the default), the drawings are saved to
.I ~/.config/Screenator/screenator-annotations.png
when Screenator quits and restored on the next start; changed papers are
offered for saving on exit. "Clear Screen" removes the saved drawing.
.br
A single, colourful Hints dialog appears when Screenator starts. It is a
700x500 scrollable column that summarises the common mouse and stylus controls
plus shape-editing tips, the global F9 shortcut and the Screen and Paper mode
shortcuts in one place instead of repeating them for every tool.
A "Never show hints again" option
beside the Close button is remembered across restarts. "Show Hints" in the
menu opens the dialog again, and Reset app settings and drawings keeps the
choice. The hints text is loaded from the installed HTML file each time the
dialog opens; it is not edited after installation. Headings, paragraphs,
lists, bold/italic/underline text, links and coloured spans are supported.
Simple <style> rules such as h2 { color: #008800; } and
span { color: #0000aa; } also work.
.br
.SH KEYBOARD CONTROL
By default, Screenator grabs the "F9" key globally (this can be changed using
the "\-\-key" option), making it unavailable to other applications. Pressing
.B F9
shows or hides the floating icon at any time.
.B F8
selects the Pen tool,
.B F6
selects the Arrow tool,
.B F7
selects the Text tool and
.B F12
starts the Timer.
.B F10
shows or hides the tool bar in both Screen and Paper modes. It is horizontal
on the top edge by default; Settings > General can place it vertically on the
right edge instead.
.B F3
activates the selected paper or turns it off. In Screen mode,
.B F4
saves a screenshot of the current screen, including the annotations or paper
page, to the Pictures directory as Screenator-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.png in both
Screen and Paper modes. The old Gromit-MPX
shortcuts (Shift+F9, Ctrl+F9, Alt+F9, Shift+F8 and the old F8 undo) are no
longer grabbed.
.PP
While painting mode is active, the usual
.B Ctrl+Z
and
.B Ctrl+Shift+Z
shortcuts undo and redo drawing strokes and page insertion or deletion.
Drawing undo is used first while drawing history exists. Undo history keeps 50
steps in every mode and covers the whole document: switching pages does not
clear it, and undo returns to the page and view where the step was made. Pressing
.B Esc
first finishes the current shape or closes its handles and keeps the selected
tool. Pressing it again exits the tool and selects the Cursor tool so its icon
becomes active. These keys are only grabbed while painting, so they keep their
normal meaning in other applications.
.SH PAPER ERASER CONTROLS
In Paper mode, Clear Screen clears the whole page with the largest visible
area and preserves neighboring pages. With Eraser selected, the wheel scrolls
pages. A short Button 1 (middle-click) press changes eraser size; holding and dragging it
pans the page after a brief 200 ms delay and a small movement, including while hovering.
Holding Button 2 (right-click) erases while hovering.
.SH STYLUS
.B IMPORTANT:
In your stylus configuration, set button 1 (near the tip) to Middle Click and
button 2 to Right Click.
The stylus tip draws like the left mouse button, using the selected line
width; pen pressure is ignored so it draws exactly like the mouse. The first
side button and the middle mouse button do not erase on a normal press. With
Pen selected, middle mouse does nothing.
In Screen mode, with Eraser selected, stylus side button 1 erases and side button 2 changes
its size. While a text or shape still shows its
handles, the first side button changes its colour instead. The second side
button acts like right click: it accepts text, then commits visible handles on
another click before a later click erases, without exiting the tool. In Screen mode, with
Eraser selected it changes
the eraser size. There is no side-button popup menu. In Paper mode the buttons
change roles: hold side button 1 or the middle mouse button to pan, and hold
side button 2 or the right mouse button to erase. Middle click never erases.
Eraser and Paint Bucket panning use a 200 ms delay; other tools pan immediately; releasing the right button or
side button 2 brings the previous tool back. A middle click accepts typed text
or commits handles before panning. The mouse wheel scrolls the pages except
while text is being typed or handles are edited, when it changes colour.
The hints dialog
covers the stylus controls at startup and can be reopened with "Show Hints".
.SH OPTIONS (STARTUP)
A short summary of the available command line arguments for starting Screenator;
see below for the options to control an already running Screenator process:
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-active
start Screenator and immediately activate it.
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-debug
gives some debug output.
.TP
.B \-k <keysym>, \-\-key <keysym>
change the global key used to show or hide the floating icon. <keysym> can
e.g. be "F9", "F12", "Control_R" or "Print". To determine the keysym for
different keys you can use the \fBxev\fP(1) command. You can specify "none"
to prevent Screenator from grabbing a key.
.TP
.B \-K <keycode>, \-\-keycode <keycode>
change the floating-icon key by keycode instead of keysym.
.TP
.B \-o, \-\-opacity <value>
set the initial drawing opacity using a value between 0 and 1.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
show the Screenator version.
.SH OPTIONS (CONTROL)
A short summary of the available command line arguments to control an already
running Screenator process:
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-clear
clear the screen.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quit
cause the running Screenator process to quit.
.TP
.B \-t, \-\-toggle
toggle the grabbing of the pointer.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-visibility
toggle the visibility of the drawing.
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-reload
reload the tool configuration.
.TP
.B \-y, \-\-redo
redo the last undone drawing stroke or page operation.
.TP
.B \-z, \-\-undo
undo the last drawing stroke or page operation.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP
Screenator uses this to determine which desktop environment it is running on.
.TP
.B XDG_CURRENT_SESSION
Screenator uses this to determine whether it is running under X11 or Wayland.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Directory to search for the user's configuration files, defaults to
.BI ~ /.config/ .
.TP
.B GDK_CORE_DEVICE_EVENTS
If set, GDK does not use the XInput extension and only reacts to core X input
events, which renders Screenator unusable.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/Screenator/screenator.cfg
User tool configuration: defines pens and maps mouse buttons and modifiers to
them.
.TP
.I /etc/screenator/screenator.cfg
System-wide tool configuration, used when the user configuration does not
exist.
.TP
.I ~/.config/Screenator/screenator.ini
Saved menu settings (line colour, thickness, opacity, shape fill, Paper
Drawing page style, floating icon size, settings indicator visibility, whether annotations
are saved on exit, text font, math expression and range, and the selected
tool).
.TP
.I ~/.config/Screenator/screenator-annotations.png
The drawing layer saved when Screenator quits, restored on the next start.
.SH NOTES
Screenator is X11-only. Under Wayland it runs through XWayland.
